{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1455307-a-very-cold-thing-al-asgard\u2019s-trace-in-mage","id":1455307,"title":"A very cold thing: Al-Asgard\u2019s trace in Mage","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2021-07-21T08:40:59-07:00","id":1455307,"title":"A very cold thing: Al-Asgard\u2019s trace in Mage","content":"Very accidental though. <br /> <br /> Guide to the Tradition, Page 91 <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Most of the records of this period come from oral folklore, handed down among Arabian and Persian sorcerers, and a few cryptic Hebrew documents the Order of Hermes holds on to. Supposedly Shaitan \u2014 or Baal or Set, depending upon whom you ask \u2014 unleashed the 10,000 Djinni as a plague upon the Earth. The sheer magnitude of the problem led to an alliance among various Hebrew, Egyptian and Babylonian priests and demon hunters. Under the leadership of Suleiman the Wise, King of Israel; <b>al-Ashrad the Mighty, a demon hunter from the Arabian deserts;</b> and Abana-ta-sherit, leader of the Egyptian House of the Sestat, the alliance managed to bind or destroy the majority of the Djinni. However, this group was only an alliance of convenience and, once the threat had passed, the various sects went their separate ways. No group formed solely on the basis of a common enemy has any hope of holding together in the long run. Take away the threat and they fall apart. </div> </div> </div> He is the one of main leaders fighting 1000 Dijinni Plague (Happened between BC 945-930, Shaitan (or Baal, or Set) unleashed a multitude of Djinni as a plague upon the Earth)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":24560,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24560-rock113","user":"Rock113","date":". 07-21-2021, 12:16 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24560-rock113","name":"Rock113","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24560_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"720"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-21T10:18:19-07:00","id":1455313,"title":"","content":"It is indeed a nice reference to the Amr. Nice that you caught that, never saw it tbh.<br /> It does prove that he has been around for a while and that his story of beating a child of Haqim in the first century CE is very plausible if he would be almost 1000 years old by that time.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26925-denstoffy","name":"Denstoffy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26925_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"33"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-21T11:54:57-07:00","id":1455318,"title":"","content":"But TBH he maybe a little young at that time, even if he is born in 1000 BC (less than 100 year old, and became one of main leaders?\u2026). Maybe author simply thought his name appears here is a cool idea and did it &gt;_","signature":"","edited":{"userid":24560,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24560-rock113","user":"Rock113","date":". 07-21-2021, 02:59 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24560-rock113","name":"Rock113","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24560_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"720"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-21T12:26:06-07:00","id":1455319,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Rock113</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1455307-a-very-cold-thing-al-asgard\u2019s-trace-in-mage?p=1455318#post1455318\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But TBH he maybe a little young at that time, even if he is born in 1000 BC (less than 100 year old, and became one of main leaders?\u2026). Maybe author simply thought his name appears here is a cool idea and did it &gt;_</div> </div> </div> </div>My math could be off but if he was slaying demons 1000 BCE and he is sired the first century CE, doesn't that make him 1000+ years old however you spin it when he becomes the child of Ur-Shulgi?<br /> <br /> And with that rule that you can transfer half your mage xp to thaumaturgy like things, that would kinda make him a powerhouse in no time. Basically what the Tremere dit a thousand years later? <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/cool.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Cool\" smilieid=\"6\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26925-denstoffy","name":"Denstoffy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26925_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"33"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-21T12:40:41-07:00","id":1455321,"title":"","content":"Well, I mean he is one of leaders in fighting 10000 Dijinni Plague (945-930 BC), he maybe a little young even if he is born in 1000 BC (less than 100 year old)?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24560-rock113","name":"Rock113","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24560_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"720"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-23T08:39:02-07:00","id":1455442,"title":"","content":"Pretty sure (going by memory) Children of the Night says he was already a mage and very long-lived at the time of his Embrace, but doesn't give any hard dates. Al-Ashrad could've been a young man or ancient when he fought the dijinni. <br /> <br /> I've always seen mages as developing their powers faster than vampires, anyways. Immortality isn't their default option. Most of them die within a mortal lifetime. <br /> <br /> This is cool to see in a Mage book, anyway. Did not remember it was a thing.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://blood-and-bourbon.obsidianportal.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Blood and Bourbon</a>, my New Orleans-based Vampire chronicle.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3110-false-epiphany","name":"False Epiphany","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3110_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"748"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-23T11:30:01-07:00","id":1455453,"title":"","content":"Yeah Mage improvement is broken with Vampire standard &gt;_&lt; a mortal-age one can have elder-level sheer power, a elder-age one can almost destroy Death itself, a ante-age one can really f**k the whole Creation.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24560-rock113","name":"Rock113","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24560_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"720"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-23T13:55:03-07:00","id":1455464,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Rock113</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1455307-a-very-cold-thing-al-asgard\u2019s-trace-in-mage?p=1455453#post1455453\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yeah Mage improvement is broken with Vampire standard &gt;_&lt; a mortal-age one can have elder-level sheer power, a elder-age one can almost destroy Death itself, a ante-age one can really f**k the whole Creation.</div> </div> </div> </div>It probably helps that Mages 1) have a more freeform and spontaneous way of creating needed Effects (b/c of Spheres) that a vampire would need to spend lots of time/XP learning piecemeal, 2) have a time pressure to develop as quickly as possible due to their mortal lifespan, and 3) can benefit from written texts and mentorship that most (non-sorcerous) vampires cannot.<br /> <br /> So Mages are very suited to burning fast and bright, at least by vampire standards. Vampires, meanwhile, must develop their power slowly. Very few Mages reach the tipping point where they're both growing fast and living forever.<br /> <br /> There are advantages Vampires have that Mages don't, though. Setting aside Paradox - very generous indeed - Vampires can produce Vampire underlings far faster than Mages can train new Mages or even just Sorcerers. Mages have the bottleneck of natural (or, more rarely, Grimoire-assisted) Awakening to check the rate of them coming into existence. Moreover, they need to then be found, if a given existing Mage is to make use of them. Whereas Vampires can create more Vampires very easily, and have them on hand throughout the whole process. Sorcerers are more plentiful because they don't need to be Awakened, but require years of training to be an equal to a newbie Mage. Whereas Vampires can produce Ghouls as quickly as they can obtain blood.<br /> <br /> None of this is to say that it's wise for a Vampire to produce loads and loads of Childer and Ghouls at once. It just means they could if they wanted to. And that they will naturally have more minions at their disposal under &quot;normal&quot; circumstances than a Mage will. (On the matter of cultists or mind-controlled minions, this is a wash. Many of the same tricks available to one group are available to the other, so neither pulls ahead in this regard. We're talking about minions who, themselves, have supernatural power.) Combine this with a Vampire's long unlife, and they have more opportunities to build strong power bases, at least until the Mage develops life extension.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-08-01T02:54:54-07:00","id":1455916,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1455307-a-very-cold-thing-al-asgard\u2019s-trace-in-mage?p=1455464#post1455464\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So Mages are very suited to burning fast and bright, at least by vampire standards. Vampires, meanwhile, must develop their power slowly. Very few Mages reach the tipping point where they're both growing fast and living forever.<br /> (...)<br /> Combine this with a Vampire's long unlife, and they have more opportunities to build strong power bases, at least until the Mage develops life extension.</div> </div> </div> </div>While the setting description is lacking in this regard, I think that life extension is actually readily available to Mages. One detail that points to this is in the Progenitors 1st edition Convention Book. There is a Procedure called <b>Nanotech Life Support</b>, and it is noted that one of the younger Progenitors has received it, and it is well beyond her Spheres ratings. So it is possible for Mages to receive life extension from their Mage faction. Also - though I don't remember where it is detailed - the Progenitors have produced drugs and substances which halt aging, though with some caveats (like disrupting some body functions, though I think it is only the lesser version of this life extension that does it). If the Technocracy can do it, I see no reason why the Tradition Mages or the Nephandi can't do it for their younger Mages, or those who don't have the requisite Spheres.<br /> <br /> It just makes no sense to me, that all these Arete 3-5 Mages described in the books are 20-50 years away from passing. For example, there is this Mage, Branwyn Elspeth, and as per her Sphere ratings she is of the seventh rank (out of ten ranks) in the Order of Hermes, Adeptus Maior. I don't really see her - or the much older Chantry leader - growing old. Surely their Tradition would lend them some Effects or magical items to subdue or at least slow aging.<br /> Or maybe it could be that it is an innate quality of being a Mage that they can reach the age of 150-300 years without problems? They grow as old as they feel like, some staying young as if in their 20s or 30s, while others stop at an older physical appearance.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3254,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3254-muad-dib","user":"Muad'Dib","date":". 08-02-2021, 09:42 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3254-muad-dib","name":"Muad'Dib","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3254_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2059"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-08-01T15:44:01-07:00","id":1455964,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Muad'Dib</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1455307-a-very-cold-thing-al-asgard\u2019s-trace-in-mage?p=1455916#post1455916\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">While the setting description is lacking in this regard, I think that life extension is actually readily available to Mages. One detail that points to this is in the Progenitors 1st edition Convention Book. There is a Procedure called <b>Nanotech Life Support</b>, and it is noted that one of the younger Progenitors has received it, and it is well beyond her Spheres ratings. So it is possible for Mages to receive life extension from their Mage faction. Also - though I don't remember where it is detailed - the Progenitors have produced drugs and substances which halt aging, though with some caveats (like disrupting some body functions, though I think it is only the lesser version of this life extension that does it). If the Technocracy can do it, I see no reason why the Tradition Mages or the Nephandi can't do it for their younger Mages, or those who don't have the requisite Spheres.<br /> <br /> It just makes no sense to me, that all these Arete 3-5 Mages described in the books are 20-50 years away from passing. For example, there is this Mage, Branwyn Elspeth, and as per her Sphere ratings she is of the seventh rank in the Order of Hermes, Adeptus Maior. I don't really see her - or the much older Chantry leader - growing old. Surely their Tradition would lend them some Effects or magical items to subdue or at least slow aging.<br /> Or maybe it could be that it is an innate quality of being a Mage that they can reach the age of 150-300 years without problems? They grow as old as they feel like, some staying young as if in their 20s or 30s, while others stop as an older appearance.</div> </div> </div> </div>First, the Technocracy is a united organization. Pooling their resources is part of the advantage they have over the frequently fractious Traditions. It's not always possible for Tradition mages to spend time, effort, and resources keeping every single member of their alliance alive.<br /> <br /> Second, as an extension of the above, even the Technocracy is not liable to permit these life extension procedures/drugs to just any member of the Union. The fact that they're a big organization means they have far greater control over where resources get allocated. They'd be selective about who gets to have their life extended. If any random Technocrat wants to live longer, they need to make themselves useful enough to the Union that they're worth the time and effort. (That, or the agent is expected to start learning Life procedures themselves, which can distract from other projects or studies that may be more important to them.)<br /> <br /> Third, going back to the Traditions, because they're less united, both between and within the nine Traditions, the distribution of Mages who have sufficient ranks in Life to allow for life extension (much less that can perform it on others) is going to be uneven. It's entirely reasonable to assume the Verbana have plentiful access to life extension. Not as much for other Traditions, and in particular factions within those Traditions that focus hard on non-Life Spheres. If you're a Virtual Adept that works mostly with Correspondence and Forces focused Adepts, you'll have to go further afield if you want some quality anti-aging.<br /> <br /> Fourth, even where access to life extension exists within a network, that stuff isn't necessarily going to come cheap. Tradition Mages are not only frequently strapped for resources (money, Tass, knowledge, etc), they also have their own work to do. Even Time masters only have so many weeks in a day to attend to someone else's business. Which is not to say you don't see Mages giving each other helping hands, especially for matters of life and death. You're just more likely to see them trading favors or resources. Youth potions are Charms that cost Tass to produce (or else requires the Mage themselves to be on hand to administer, which is even more imposing on their time). That kind of thing isn't cheap. There's inevitably going to be a bottleneck somewhere.<br /> <br /> (Plus, not every Mage likes each other. The most readily available Life mage one has may be one's greatest rival.)<br /> <br /> <br /> None of this is to say that trading life extension doesn't happen. It's just that it's not as easy as it would appear on paper, just looking at the Spheres involved. Whereas with Vampires, so long as the blood keeps rolling in and they aren't killed, Immortality is the default. Being able to live forever isn't a separate problem on top of immediate or long-term survival. They do not have the same bottleneck as Mages, either in knowledge or resources or connections.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-08-01T21:40:32-07:00","id":1455991,"title":"","content":"If you look at a lot of the NPCs in First Edition, you find more than a few who are over a century old, and some who are several. Porthos was (IIRC) 590 back when the original game came out. Nightshade was at least 600 when she decided to let herself die. Senex is/was at least 500. IIRC, Life 3 or 4 allows one to slow down their aging process and regenerate the damage that comes from it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-08-02T05:20:19-07:00","id":1456004,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1455307-a-very-cold-thing-al-asgard\u2019s-trace-in-mage?p=1455964#post1455964\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">First, the Technocracy is a united organization. Pooling their resources is part of the advantage they have over the frequently fractious Traditions. It's not always possible for Tradition mages to spend time, effort, and resources keeping every single member of their alliance alive.<br /> <br /> (\u2026)<br /> <br /> (Plus, not every Mage likes each other. The most readily available Life mage one has may be one's greatest rival.)<br /> <br /> <br /> None of this is to say that trading life extension doesn't happen. It's just that it's not as easy as it would appear on paper, just looking at the Spheres involved. Whereas with Vampires, so long as the blood keeps rolling in and they aren't killed, Immortality is the default. Being able to live forever isn't a separate problem on top of immediate or long-term survival. They do not have the same bottleneck as Mages, either in knowledge or resources or connections.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is a nice analysis, though I still hold to the idea that Traditions (and the Nephandi..considering their lower numbers*, they certainly couldn't afford for their Mages to pass due to old age) do look after each other to a larger extent in regards to longevity, than you presume. Also - next to Life and Time, Entropy and Prime could also help with life extension. Possibly through allowing Conjunctional Magick that would lower the Spheres required.<br /> <br /> *Does anyone have this word file which broke down the probable numbers of Nightfolk? I wonder how exactly the numbers got worked out. I know that for example the Virtual Adepts and Dreamspeakers Tradition Books give some hints as to how many Mages there are.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1455307-a-very-cold-thing-al-asgard\u2019s-trace-in-mage?p=1455964#post1455964\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There are advantages Vampires have that Mages don't, though. Setting aside Paradox - very generous indeed - Vampires can produce Vampire underlings far faster than Mages can train new Mages or even just Sorcerers. Mages have the bottleneck of natural (or, more rarely, Grimoire-assisted) Awakening to check the rate of them coming into existence. Moreover, they need to then be found, if a given existing Mage is to make use of them. Whereas Vampires can create more Vampires very easily, and have them on hand throughout the whole process. Sorcerers are more plentiful because they don't need to be Awakened, but require years of training to be an equal to a newbie Mage. Whereas Vampires can produce Ghouls as quickly as they can obtain blood.<br /> <br /> None of this is to say that it's wise for a Vampire to produce loads and loads of Childer and Ghouls at once. It just means they could if they wanted to. And that they will naturally have more minions at their disposal under &quot;normal&quot; circumstances than a Mage will. (On the matter of cultists or mind-controlled minions, this is a wash. Many of the same tricks available to one group are available to the other, so neither pulls ahead in this regard. We're talking about minions who, themselves, have supernatural power.) Combine this with a Vampire's long unlife, and they have more opportunities to build strong power bases, at least until the Mage develops life extension.</div> </div> </div> </div>In regard to advantages of Vampires, I think the ability to Blood Bond certainly gives them edge in creating retainers, contacts, minions, and agents. I think that in setting a Mage applying Mind 4 or Mind 5 Effects is both harder to achieve (especially at a moment\u2019s notice), and produce lesser outcome relatively to the simplicity of the Blood Bond.<br /> Talking about influencing the mind and emotions, I think that Vampire Disciplines have the edge here. Dominate, for example, is implied in the <i>Revised Ventrue Clanbook</i> to give the ability to access the memories and thoughts of other persons. In regard to Presence, I recently played <i>VtM: Coteries of the New York</i>, and the Toreador patron of the PC (Sophie Langlay) is constantly going around on Awe (or Majesty), and her aura of charisma is very often noted to be so strong that I doubt Mages could easily achieve a similar result as to swaying other persons.<br /> <br /> My point is that the mind influencing abilities of Vampires seem to have a potency that is available at a moment\u2019s notice, that Mages can\u2019t really achieve barring long rituals. Mages can win out when they can prepare and plan, while Vampires have their power available at all times. Mages have versatility, Vampires have sheer supernatural power.<br /> <br /> I agree that in the long term, the ability to sire new Vampires and to create Ghouls is a definite edge that Vampires have over Mages. Even a humble Ghoul with level 1 Disciplines can accomplish some vital objectives if given proper guidelines.<br /> <br /> I like your view point that Vampires have immortality granted (even as I disagree as to the details in regard to Mages' life extension) \u2013 certainly it is an interesting consideration when comparing different Nightfolk. One noteworthy detail is that Ghouls are also in practice immortal - even a relatively young Vampire (say, 40 or 60 years of Unlife) can have servants who have experienced a lot and are prepared to go the extra mile for the Vampire because of it. And Ghouls of Vampire Ancillae and Elders (and Methuselahs) are on another level completely.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3254,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3254-muad-dib","user":"Muad'Dib","date":". 08-02-2021, 09:44 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3254-muad-dib","name":"Muad'Dib","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3254_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2059"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-08-02T11:14:45-07:00","id":1456019,"title":"","content":"Mage can live centuries with standard Sphere magics. Masters of Art has two most popular Rotes (demanding though), but both are flawed.<br /> <br /> Live for thousands of years may need something extreme. <br /> Rotes like Lichedom in Dead Magic which makes you half-dead. <br /> Maraudism likes Medea.<br /> Great Patronage likes Unnamed who was claimed by powerful evil spirits.<br /> Other supernatural ways like Ghouldom or Mummism (all even more flawed though).<br /> <br /> Canon does have some millennial-old mages, most of them are hardly written though. Except Medea and Unnamed, Wu Kung and Wu Lung have some iirc.<br /> <br /> Comparing with Tremere, I always think some down-to-earth organizations still have same-age ones, Technoracy Controls for example. <br /> He thought no mage can live so long just because he didn\u2019t see those really old ones, who prefer to move in Umbra and focus on Ascension.<br /> <br /> Vampires have sheer powers, but with preparation mages can do something really impressive (Al-ashrad prepared and defeat several 4th gens) with younger age, their upper limit may be much higher, too, no vampires can really return someone to human for example.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24560-rock113","name":"Rock113","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24560_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"720"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-08-09T17:11:36-07:00","id":1456475,"title":"","content":"Life extension is one of those things that can vary depending on how its achieved (linear magics might be able to get around the limits True mages have such as through alchemy) and we know of merits that give long life or immortality even without true magic extensions so I don't see it as a hard and fast rule. Heck, 1st edition WOD had Talaq the 'human' assamite (who still had vampire powers) achieve that through his deal with the kabbalist mage (who also got immortality in exchange as I recall). I'm also pretty sure the middle east is a different prospect entirely when it comes to Consensus than the Western world is which is also going to be a factor without the chronological factor (the Consensus related troubles didn't occur until 1000 BCE IIRC, and we didn't see serious problems - the ones that caused the Tremere to investigate vampirism - until ~1000 CE. And that again was just in the western world.) It strikes me there's plenty of wiggle room to explain/justify such things without much problem depending on the sources you're willing to engage (Especially if it involves crossovers of sources.) <br /> <br /> In that same vein, the Vampire vs Mage dispute is also crossover context dependent - especially if you factor in the opinions of 'crossover' comments in various sources (including mage vs Vampire books like Blood Treachery or Red Sign) you face the fundamental differences between the two systems narratively before you get into the mechanics (such as Technocracy being incapable of taking down even one antediluvian collectively despite.. being mages. I feel that alone could argue there's significant differences between the Technocratic union as envisioned by VtM and the Union in Mage proper.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25533-mister_dunpeal","name":"Mister_Dunpeal","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25533_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"308"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}